[cot:thought] now clear. Next I will examine a dashboard component for layout patterns. The topic compares correspondent banking to instant rails for retail FX.
[cot:thought] The headline will be rail share of retail FX. BIS CPMI, World Bank
[cot:thought] GPSS, and central bank data will anchor the analysis. Plausible desk estimates will draw on known public facts: correspondent banking still dominates retail cross-border FX, while instant domestic rails like PIX, UPI, and FedNow are expanding for domestic and bilateral linkages. Key references include CPMI cross-border payment targets for the G20 and World Bank remittance cost-speed comparisons.
[cot:thought] SWIFT dominates cross-border
[cot:thought] messaging. PIX and UPI are massive national systems but global instant rail share remains small at roughly one to fifteen percent of retail remittance value. Correspondent banking still clears most retail FX. The G20 target is cheaper, faster, and more transparent cross-border payments by 2027. I am now structuring the data-rich post.
[cot:thought] The post will feature
[cot:thought] six panels: global retail cross-border value by rail type, corridor-level share comparisons, time series of FPS-linked share growth, a cost-speed scatter, regional stacked composition, and controls for region, metric mode, and year. Chart types will include stacked area, grouped bar, scatter, donut or pie, and line. I will check a recent dashboard for UI patterns without copying its payment content.
